{#
	One module the customer already has, as the store names it back to them
	(XIV-140).

	**Small on purpose.** These are the majority of the page for an established
	customer and the least interesting thing on it, so they get a dense row rather
	than the tile an offer gets. Still a card, because "you have this" is an answer
	and an answer needs somewhere to sit.

	**Their word for it, not the blueprint's.** §6.1 stops the blueprint having a
	say the moment a module is installed, so a customer who renamed Contacts to
	Klienten reads Klienten here, exactly as they do in the bar at the top of every
	page. The other half of the screen uses the blueprint's label, because nothing
	over there is theirs to have renamed yet.

	**A link only while the store still has a page for it.** A module withdrawn
	from the store, or dropped from a build, is still installed and still works
	(§6.2), and `store_module` answers 404 for it. Same rule, same reason, as the
	requirement links on a module's own page: a link to a page that refuses is
	worse than a name that sits still.
#}
<div class="col-6 col-md-4 col-xl-3">
	<div class="card h-100">
		<div class="card-body d-flex align-items-center gap-2 py-2 px-3">
			<i class="bi bi-{{ module.icon }} text-body-secondary" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			<span class="me-auto text-truncate">
				{% if module.offered %}
					<a class="stretched-link text-reset text-decoration-none"
					   href="{{ path('store_module', {module: module.key}) }}">{{ module.label }}</a>
				{% else %}
					{{ module.label }}
				{% endif %}
			</span>
			<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ 'store.installed'|trans }}</span>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
